<!doctype html>
<!--
  The Seedbed.

  Six drills of eight places across a bed of fine tilth, forty-eight to a plot —
  the Long Walk's number, where the Knot Garden holds thirty-two, the Crossing
  twenty-four, the Orchard twenty and the Quiet Garden ten, all in the same
  square.

  **This is the first area whose rule groups by sameness.** The five before it
  all sort by difference: the walk grades a border by height, the Quiet Garden
  reads a group of three, the Crossing ranks a quarter, the Orchard stands its
  tallest in the middle, the Knot Garden claims a pair by colour and then grades
  it. Every one asks *how does this plant differ from that one*. A nursery row
  asks the other question, and a drill sown with one kind is the plainest answer
  to it.

  **A kind is the epithet, and that was measured rather than chosen.** Over five
  hundred crossings 490 binomials are unique and the genus is nearly as rare, so
  a drill claimed by either would hold one plant and wait forever. The epithet
  repeats — 46 of them over those five hundred — and it is the truer reading
  besides, because an epithet is chosen to say the one thing that is most so
  about a plant where a genus is inherited.

  **A drill is claimed, never reserved**, as the Knot Garden's pairs are: the
  first plant sown in a drill claims it for its kind, and only that kind joins
  it. Nothing is held open for a kind that has not arrived, which is what keeps
  a bed of 46 possible kinds from owing more drills than it has.

  **So a bed is mostly part-sown, and the page says so rather than hiding it.**
  A new plot opens the moment a seventh kind arrives, and it opens holding one
  plant. Five hundred arrivals fill 65% of the places here against the Knot
  Garden's 92%, and that waste is the right waste: a kind that arrives once
  stands in its drill alone. The six drills are written out under the drawing,
  each with its kind and how many of its eight places are sown, because the
  labels in the bed cannot carry it — nothing is written on a row label, at this
  scale a word would be four pixels tall and fight the plants.

  **This page is the one the app talks to, not a picture of it.** The plants are
  grown here, in the browser, from the same SeedCore that grows them on the
  phone — `plant.js` loads that library built for wasm32 and `seedbed.js` hands
  it each planting the plot service holds. Nothing is drawn from an image and
  nothing is precomputed: a plant on this page is the plant on the phone because
  it is the same arithmetic, not because somebody kept two drawings in step. The
  row labels come from the same place, through `Organic`.

  **The sky is the real one**, worked out for wherever this browser is keeping
  time and asking for nothing to find out — `sky.js`, a port of SeedCore's
  `Sky`, held to it by `tools/reference/check_sky.mjs`. Both halves of it: the
  plots float, so the stars under them are drawn too.

  **It is a night, so the page is dark whatever the reader's system says.**
  Every other page here follows `prefers-color-scheme`; this one cannot,
  because the garden is lit by its own sky and a pale page around it is a
  second room.

  `noindex`, for the reason `/g` gives: walkable and indexed are different
  properties and only one of them was asked for.

  Static, like the rest: no analytics, no font CDN, no third-party anything.
-->
<!-- `data-module` is stamped by index.php: the module's address with this
     build's mark on it, so a new one is a new address. -->
<html lang="en" data-module="PG_MODULE">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover" />
    <title>Peace Garden — the Seedbed</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Six drills across a bed of fine tilth, each sown with one kind of the plants two people grew." />
    <meta name="color-scheme" content="dark" />
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/site.css" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/assets/icon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/assets/icon-180.png" />
  </head>

  <body class="night">
    <!--
      The plot itself, behind everything. The sky is behind it, and the pool of
      light sits between them so the plot stands in front of the sky rather than
      on top of a picture of one.
    -->
    <div class="walk" aria-hidden="true">
      <canvas id="sky"></canvas>
      <div class="pool"></div>
    </div>

    <main class="page walk-page" id="page">
      <!--
        The bar. On every page of the site, and here it says one more thing: the
        link back to the map carries this area in its fragment, and `/g` reads
        one — so coming back from an area lands in that area rather than at the
        top of a map the reader has to find their place in again. `gates.js`
        writes the fragment.

        **It does not name the area.** The heading under the plot names it, and
        the same words twice on one screen is one time too many.
      -->
      <header class="masthead bar">
        <a class="bar__home" href="/">
          <img class="mark" src="/assets/icon.svg" alt="" width="44" height="44" />
          <span class="wordmark">Peace Garden</span>
        </a>
        <span class="bar__ways">
          <a class="quiet-button" href="/garden" id="bar-hub" data-s="gardenTitle"></a>
        </span>
      </header>

      <!--
        The drawing, in the page's own flow rather than fixed behind it. The
        words sit under it, which is the arrangement `PlantDetailView` uses in
        the app. See site.css §An area page.
      -->
      <canvas class="walk-stage" id="stage" aria-label="The Seedbed"></canvas>

      <h1 class="heading" data-s="areaBeginnings"></h1>
      <p class="body walk-about" data-s="seedbedAbout"></p>

      <!--
        The six drills, written by `seedbedpage.js`: the kind that claimed each
        one, in the serif this site keeps for the name a plant is given, and how
        many of its eight places are sown — as eight marks rather than as a
        number, the way `/g` draws how full an area is. A number would be a
        string and this is the same fact in no language at all.

        **All six, claimed or not.** A bed is drawn out and labelled before
        anything goes into it and stands part-sown long afterwards. Listing only
        the drills with something in them would make a bed of one row look full.
      -->
      <ul class="drills" id="drills" hidden></ul>

      <!--
        One line, and only when there is something to say: while the plants are
        being grown, or when there is nothing here yet. It carries no count — a
        running tally of plots and plants is a readout, and this is a garden.
      -->
      <p class="label walk-note" id="note" role="status" hidden></p>

      <!--
        What gathers here: the theme this area is, the syllables that bring a
        plant to it, and its three parts, with a way to the whole table. Written
        by `meanings.js` from the one table `/meanings` is drawn from, so the two
        cannot come to disagree. Hidden until it is written, so a page whose
        script has not run shows nothing rather than an empty band.
      -->
      <section class="gathers" id="gathers" hidden></section>

      <div class="walk-ways">
        <nav class="walk-keys" id="keys" role="group" hidden>
          <!--
            Two pairs, and the gap between them says so: paging from one plot
            to the next, then turning the one in front of you. They used to be
            four words in a row, and four equal buttons read as four unrelated
            actions however they were ordered.

            **Glyphs, and the words are their names.** `data-s-label` has
            `plain.js` write each one's `aria-label` and tooltip from the
            catalogue, so a screen reader hears *Turn anticlockwise* in the
            reader's own language. The chevrons are previous and next, and
            mirror under a right-to-left language as the row does; the rings
            are a direction of turn, which is the same in every language, and
            do not. Monoline, even weight, round free ends, per BRAND.md §3.2.
          -->
          <span class="walk-keys__pair">
            <button class="pad-key" type="button" id="back" data-s-label="walkBack">
              <svg class="pad-glyph pad-glyph--page" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                <path d="M12.75 4.5L7.25 10L12.75 15.5" />
              </svg>
            </button>
            <button class="pad-key" type="button" id="on" data-s-label="walkOn">
              <svg class="pad-glyph pad-glyph--page" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                <path d="M7.25 4.5L12.75 10L7.25 15.5" />
              </svg>
            </button>
          </span>
          <span class="walk-keys__pair">
            <button class="pad-key" type="button" id="left" data-s-label="walkTurnAnti">
              <svg class="pad-glyph" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                <path d="M6.27 4.68A6.5 6.5 0 1 0 13.73 4.68M16.96 4.34L13.73 4.68L14.07 7.91" />
              </svg>
            </button>
            <button class="pad-key" type="button" id="right" data-s-label="walkTurnClock">
              <svg class="pad-glyph" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                <path d="M13.73 4.68A6.5 6.5 0 1 1 6.27 4.68M5.93 7.91L6.27 4.68L3.04 4.34" />
              </svg>
            </button>
          </span>
        </nav>

        <!--
          The garden's map, small: ten cells five by two, this area filled, the
          other built areas lit and each a link to its page, the closed ones
          faint and inert. Drawn by `gates.js` from `garden.js`'s own table, so
          there is one map rather than a second one written out here.
        -->
        <nav class="minimap" id="minimap" hidden></nav>
      </div>


      <!--
        The foot mirrors the bar: one hairline, above it here where the
        bar's is below, and a row of two things at opposite ends — the
        privacy notice where the mark stands, the language where the way back
        to the garden does.
      -->
      <footer class="foot bar-foot">
        <a class="bar-foot__link" href="/privacy" data-s="privacyTitle"></a>
        <label class="chooser">
          <span class="label" id="language-label"></span>
          <span class="chooser__field">
            <select id="language" class="chooser__select"></select>
            <!-- Monoline, even weight, round free ends, per BRAND.md §3.2. -->
            <svg class="chooser__mark" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
              <path
                d="M4 6.5 L8 10.5 L12 6.5"
                fill="none"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-width="1.25"
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
              />
            </svg>
          </span>
        </label>
      </footer>
    </main>

    <script type="module" src="/assets/js/plain.js"></script>
    <script type="module" src="/assets/js/seedbedpage.js"></script>
  </body>
</html>
